  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 143 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nThe cooler and wetter pattern continues through the end of the week. \nSoutheasterly winds at lower levels will pump moisture from the Gulf \ntowards our region, while southerly to southwesterly winds aloft \ncontinue to advect mid-to-upper level moisture our direction. As a \nresult, PWATs remain between the 90th-99th percentile. This, \ncombined with lift from shortwaves in the flow aloft, will continue \nto encourage medium to high shower and thunderstorm chances across \nmainly the western half of the area Thursday and Friday (50-80%). \nThe highest chances/most rainfall is still forecast for our \nsoutheast New Mexico counties and in Culberson County, where the \nFlood Watch mentioned in the Short Term discussion remains in effect \nthrough 7AM CDT/6AM MDT Thursday (though this may be extended in \nfuture forecast updates). Chances are generally be lower for the \neastern half of the area (20-40%). \n\nBy Saturday the flow aloft becomes more zonal as ridging begins to \nbuild over the area again. As a result, the best rain chances shift \ntowards the higher terrain south and west of the Pecos (30-60% \nchances), with lower chances elsewhere. Temperatures also start to \nincrease again during this timeframe. Whereas highs in the mid-to-\nupper 80s and low 90s will be prevalent through Friday, highs in the \nlow-to-mid 90s return to most locations this weekend and into early \nnext week. By the early-to-middle part of next week, long range \nmodels continue to depict another trough moving across the Plains. \nThis trough may help push another front our direction, and may also \nallow rain chances to increase across the area again. Nevertheless, \nconfidence in the positioning, timing, and strength of the trough \nremains low this far out, so stay tuned for future updates!\n\nSprang",
